 {# Key Takeaways #}
 <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-xl p-6 mb-8 speakable">
 <h2 class="text-lg font-black uppercase text-amber-700 mb-3">Key Takeaways</h2>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>GaN chargers are 40% smaller and 30% lighter</strong> than equivalent silicon chargers, a 65W GaN charger fits in a pocket; a 65W silicon charger fills a palm. This directly reduces freight cost per unit by 25-30% for container shipping.</li>
 <li><strong>Efficiency gap: GaN achieves 93-95% vs 83-85% for silicon</strong> at full load. The 10% efficiency delta means 10W less heat to dissipate, eliminating the need for internal heatsinks and enabling sealed, slim industrial designs.</li>
 <li><strong>GaN BOM cost premium is $1.60-2.00 per unit at OEM scale</strong>, but the total landed cost advantage (smaller packaging, lighter freight, fewer warranty returns) flips the equation at volumes above 5,000 units.</li>
 <li><strong>GaN enables 240W single-port chargers</strong> in the same volume as a 65W silicon design. Silicon hits a thermal wall at ~100W without active cooling. For enterprise laptop and multi-port hub OEMs, GaN is no longer optional.</li>
 <li><strong>Silicon still wins for sub-30W fixed-installation chargers</strong> where size and weight don't matter, wall outlets, built-in USB ports, and cost-sensitive emerging markets. Don't pay the GaN premium where you can't monetize it.</li>
 </ul>
