 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the real performance difference between GaN and silicon chargers?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">GaN semiconductors have a 3.4 eV bandgap vs 1.1 eV for silicon, enabling 3 key advantages: (1) 93-95% efficiency vs 80-85% for silicon, meaning less energy wasted as heat; (2) 40-50% smaller volume at equivalent wattage; (3) 30% cooler operation under load. For B2B buyers: GaN enables 5+ year lifespan vs 2-3 years for silicon, 0.3-0.5% vs 3-5% field return rates, and 40-60% higher retail margins.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How much smaller is a GaN charger vs silicon at the same wattage?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">A 65W GaN charger is 40-50% smaller by volume than an equivalent silicon charger. At 100W+, the size gap widens dramatically because silicon requires proportionally larger heat sinks. For OEM brands: smaller packaging means 20-25% lower per-unit shipping costs (more units per container), reduced retail shelf space, and lower packaging material costs. A 65W GaN charger weighs approximately 75-85g; silicon equivalents exceed 110g. In B2B logistics, weight equals freight cost.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">GaN vs silicon BOM cost, what is the real difference at OEM volume in 2026?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">GaN commands a 15-25% BOM premium over silicon at equivalent wattage, down from 40-50% in 2023 and falling 8-12% annually. At 65W, FOB Shenzhen pricing at 1,000 units: GaN $5.50-8.00/unit vs silicon $3.00-5.00/unit. This premium is typically recovered through 40% smaller packaging (lower freight), 0.3% vs 3.2% field return rates, and 40-60% higher retail margins.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">At what wattage should OEM brands switch from silicon to GaN?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Below 20W single-port: silicon remains cost-competitive. 20-30W: the transition zone, GaN starts to win on size. 30-65W: GaN is the recommended default for new OEM designs, the BOM premium is recovered through smaller packaging and lower return rates. 65W+: GaN is effectively mandatory for any charger targeting retail above $19.99 MSRP. Above 100W: GaN IV/V is required, silicon designs exceed practical size and thermal limits.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Does EU Ecodesign regulation effectively mandate GaN for chargers above 30W?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">EU Ecodesign 2025/2052 sets efficiency and no-load power thresholds extremely difficult to meet with silicon above 30W: minimum 88% average efficiency at 4 load points, ≤0.1W standby. GaN naturally achieves 93-95% across all load points; silicon typically hits 85% and fails the 25% and 75% load efficiency thresholds. Non-compliance = EU border rejection. For OEM brands targeting the EU market, GaN above 30W is a compliance requirement, not a premium option.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the lifecycle cost advantage of GaN vs silicon for B2B fleet deployment?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">GaN chargers deliver a 3-year TCO advantage through three compounding factors: (1) 0.3% annual field failure rate vs 3.2% for silicon, on a 10,000-unit fleet, GaN saves ~290 replacement units annually at $40-60/unit replacement cost. (2) 93-95% efficiency vs 85-88% for silicon, at 65W output, GaN wastes 4.55W as heat vs 9.75W for silicon, reducing energy cost by ~$3.50/unit/year at average electricity rates. (3) 40% smaller form factor reduces freight and warehousing costs by $0.50-1.50/unit. Over a 3-year deployment cycle, GaN's $1.50-3.00 unit price premium is recovered within 12-18 months, making it the lower-TCO choice for any fleet deployment above 500 units.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Is GaN charger safety better than silicon for commercial and fleet applications?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">GaN chargers operate 15-25°C cooler than silicon equivalents at equivalent wattage, directly reducing thermal stress on internal components and enclosure materials. GaN's higher switching frequency (100-300 kHz vs 50-100 kHz for silicon) enables more precise power regulation and faster fault detection. The lower operating temperature reduces fire risk in continuous-use commercial environments (hotel rooms, office desks, fleet vehicles) where chargers may run 24/7. For hospitality procurement managers: GaN chargers meet UL 62368-1 Annex U thermal limits with margin, while silicon equivalents frequently require derating. Insurance underwriters increasingly recognize GaN's lower thermal profile as a risk-reduction factor for commercial property coverage.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do OEM buyers decide, GaN or silicon for their next charger product line?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Decision framework: (1) Check your wattage, above 30W, GaN is the only future-proof choice. Below 20W, silicon is still viable for ultra-budget SKUs. (2) Check your target market, EU-bound chargers above 30W need GaN for Ecodesign compliance. (3) Check your margin strategy, GaN commands 40-60% higher retail margins that more than offset the 15-25% BOM premium. GaN V chargers stabilize at 58.3°C under 100% load vs 82.7°C for silicon, better thermal performance means lower warranty costs. <strong>The ROI case for GaN above 30W is now closed.</strong></p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

